dw_mmc: survive a warm restart with the card still busy

A Linux-side `reboot` left the board hung on "Waiting for root device
/dev/mmcblk0" for ever, and no number of esptool resets recovered it - only a
physical repower did.

**What happens.** Linux restarts through OpenSBI: hart1 raises an SBI system
reset, hart0 sees it and calls esp_restart() (hosted_sram.c:581). That resets
the SoC. It does *not* reset the microSD, which has its own power domain - so
whatever the card was doing when the reboot landed carries straight into the
next boot. A card interrupted mid-write holds DAT0 low, and the first thing
initialisation does is send CMD0 into that busy line:

	dw_mmc 20342000.mmc: Busy; trying anyway
	mmc_host mmc0: Timeout sending command (cmd 0x202000 arg 0x0 status 0x80202000)

repeated at 400/300/200/100 kHz until the core gives up. `rootwait` on the
command line then waits for a device that will never appear, which is why the
board looked wedged rather than reporting a fault.

**Two changes.**

*The busy wait was 500 ms.* The SD spec allows a card finishing an internal
program to hold busy for seconds, so the old timeout expired and the driver
sent CMD0 anyway - guaranteeing the timeout it then reported. It is 5 s now,
and logs how long it actually waited. This costs nothing when the card is idle:
the poll exits on its first read.

*dw_mmc-pltfm had no .shutdown.* device_shutdown() runs on the reboot path, so
that is the hook for leaving the card in a state the next boot can use. It now
tears the host down - interrupts off, controller reset, transfers stopped - so
a transfer is not in flight at the moment the SoC disappears.

**What this does not fix.** A card already stuck busy needs its power cycled;
measured here, it stayed busy across repeated EN resets for ~20 minutes and
only a repower cleared it. The 5 s wait covers a card that is genuinely
finishing work; .shutdown is what stops it being interrupted in the first
place. After a repower the card enumerated first time with no busy or timeout
lines at all.

Note for anyone editing this: `(ktime_get_ns() - t0) / NSEC_PER_MSEC` does not
link on rv32 - a 64-bit divide pulls in __udivdi3. Use div_u64().

--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c
@@ -555,11 +555,33 @@
 		u64 t0 = ktime_get_ns();
 		bool waited = !!(mci_readl(host, STATUS) & SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY);
 
+		/*
+		 * 5 s, not 500 ms.
+		 *
+		 * A card interrupted mid-write holds DAT0 low until its
+		 * internal program finishes, and the SD spec allows that to
+		 * take seconds. esp_restart() resets the SoC but not the card's
+		 * own power, so a warm reboot lands here with the card still
+		 * busy from whatever was in flight - and 500 ms was not long
+		 * enough to outlast it. The controller then sent CMD0 anyway,
+		 * which timed out, init failed, and the board sat on
+		 * "Waiting for root device /dev/mmcblk0" for ever because the
+		 * command line says rootwait.
+		 *
+		 * Waiting costs nothing when the card is idle: the poll exits
+		 * on the first read.
+		 */
 		if (readl_poll_timeout_atomic(host->regs + SDMMC_STATUS,
 					      status,
 					      !(status & SDMMC_STATUS_BUSY),
-					      10, 500 * USEC_PER_MSEC))
-			dev_err(host->dev, "Busy; trying anyway\n");
+					      10, 5 * USEC_PER_SEC))
+			dev_err(host->dev,
+				"card still busy after 5s; trying anyway\n");
+		else if (waited)
+			/* div_u64, not '/': a 64-bit divide on rv32 pulls in
+			 * __udivdi3, which the kernel does not link. */
+			dev_info(host->dev, "card was busy for %llu ms\n",
+				 div_u64(ktime_get_ns() - t0, NSEC_PER_MSEC));
 
 		dw_mci_busy_account(ktime_get_ns() - t0, waited);
 	}
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc-pltfm.c
@@ -139,9 +139,33 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(dw_mci_pltfm_remove);
 
+/*
+ * Quiesce the card before the SoC restarts.
+ *
+ * esp_restart() resets the SoC but not the microSD's own power domain, so
+ * whatever the card was doing when the reboot landed, it carries into the next
+ * boot. A card interrupted mid-write holds DAT0 low, the controller's first
+ * CMD0 then times out against a busy line, initialisation fails, and because
+ * the command line says rootwait the board waits on
+ * "Waiting for root device /dev/mmcblk0" for ever. Measured here: the card
+ * stayed busy across repeated EN resets and only a physical repower cleared it.
+ *
+ * device_shutdown() runs on the reboot path, so a .shutdown that tears the
+ * host down properly - interrupts off, controller reset, transfers stopped -
+ * is what stops a transfer being in flight at the moment the SoC disappears.
+ */
+static void dw_mci_pltfm_shutdown(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct dw_mci *host = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
+
+	if (host)
+		dw_mci_remove(host);
+}
+
 static struct platform_driver dw_mci_pltfm_driver = {
 	.probe		= dw_mci_pltfm_probe,
 	.remove		= dw_mci_pltfm_remove,
+	.shutdown	= dw_mci_pltfm_shutdown,
 	.driver		= {
 		.name		= "dw_mmc",
 		.probe_type	= PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS,
